ocation. But maybe the bug is in specific categories (e.g., Videos or Audio) or when opening the Properties via a different route. Or in the search results. Hmm.\n4. Batch overflow invert-marking inside list adapter → likely the \"Invert selection\" entry missing or doing nothing.\n\nThe hints define the changed build and likely the bugs are regressions in those areas. Let's examine each thoroughly.\n\nLet's think about the text viewer more. Amaze's text editor: in the current open-source code, `TextEditorActivity` reads file using:\n```java\nStringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();\ntry (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {\n    String line;\n    while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {\n        stringBuilder.append(line).append(\"\\n\");\n    }\n}\n```\nA chunked implementation would read e.g. 8192 chars at a time. A bug could be losing the last chunk or duplicating. Our 6.4KB content loaded correctly. Let's test a bigger file: 100KB. Hmm.\n\nCan we create a 100KB file efficiently? Maybe via the app's \"Compress\"? No. Maybe via text editor with a large paste using type_text: 100KB of text in one call might exceed tool limits. Let's test the limit gradually: we typed ~1.7KB per call fine. Could do 20 calls of 5KB = 100KB, that's 20 tool calls plus time. Possible but heavy. Let's first aim for ~20KB with 4 calls of 5KB, which covers chunk sizes up to 16KB. If still fine, maybe 64KB (13 calls). Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the seeded bug in chunked reading is about *very* large files where a full read would OOM; a chunked reader might only show the first chunk (e.g., 50KB). Typical chunk size might be 65536 or 1024*1024. Without code, choose 100KB+ to be safe? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the bug might be that for *every* text file, only a chunk is read but because our files are small no difference. Our 6.4KB loaded fully, so if chunk size were 1KB, we'd have seen truncation. So chunk size > 6.4KB, i.e., 8KB, 16KB, 64KB, or 1MB.\n\nLet me estimate typical: A chunked reader often uses a `char[] buffer = new char[8192]` or `ByteArrayOutputStream` with 8192. If the bug is \"only first chunk\", then >8KB file shows truncation. Our file is 6443 < 8192. Creating >8KB (say 12KB) is feasible: append by editing again — but cursor position issue.\n\nAlternative: create a new file and type ~12KB from scratch. That's about 7 calls of 1.7KB (same as before) — actually we typed 6.4KB in 5 calls. To reach 12KB, we can append to the existing big.txt via the editor by tapping at the end? The editor likely has a \"go to end\" feature? The overflow menu [3] may have \"Go to...\"? Let's check editor's overflow menu. Possibly includes \"Save\", \"Save as\", \"Find\", \"Go to line\", \"Word wrap\", \"Read only\"... Let's look. If \"Go to line\" exists, we can jump to last line, but our file is one line (no newlines). Hmm, we typed no newlines.\n\nBetter: while the file is open, use the Find in page to confirm tail, then use the editor's \"End\" via key? We can press key \"MOVE_END\"? The press_key supports DPAD_*, ENTER etc. Could press \"DPAD_DOWN\" many times? No.\n\nAlternative: don't append in middle. Type at beginning is fine if we include markers at start and end? If typing inserts at cursor position (likely at start of loaded text), the new content is prepended. Then the \"tail\" of file becomes our original VERY-END-MARK. If we then type a distinctive marker at the *beginning* (inserted first), our final file starts with new marker and ends with VERY-END-MARK. To detect truncation, we look for VERY-END-MARK (tail) after the file grows > chunk. Since the tail is our original end marker, truncation would omit it. Great — we don't need the cursor at end; we can prepend a big chunk and keep original tail marker. Wait but if typing inserts at cursor position not at start, e.g., cursor at 0 after load, then prepend works. If cursor is at end (unlikely for loaded text? Some editors set selection to 0), then append works. Either way, the tail marker remains somewhere: if append, tail marker is before new text; if prepend, tail marker at end. Hmm, in append case the tail is no longer at end (new text after), but we can use the new marker \"|APPEND-MARK|\" at the very end after typing... but if typing inserted at start, that marker is at start.\n\nTo be rob", "truncated": true}
