{"type": "thinking", "text": "Interesting! With 2 .txt files selected, the overflow menu shows only \"Select All\" and \"Hide\" — Share/Compress/Open with/Add Shortcut missing. Why? Perhaps because in a category page (virtual folder), many actions are unavailable; only Hide and Select All are allowed. Hmm, that changes the picture: in a category page, the batch overflow menu is limited. Maybe \"Invert Selection\" appears in the category page batch menu? It's not there either.\n\nWait, in the Amaze main folder (real path), the batch overflow showed 6 items without Invert. In the category page, only 2. Hmm.\n\nBut where would \"Invert selection\" appear? Let me check the search results batch menu. In Amaze, search results are a list where batch actions might include \"Invert selection\" because you might select all results then invert. Hmm.\n\nActually, hold on. Maybe I'm wrong about the menu contents. Let me look at Amaze's actual source for \"invert\". I recall a class `MainFragment` with `invertSelection()` method? And in `MainActivity` action mode menu, `R.id.invert_selection` might be handled. There is definitely a string \"Invert selection\" in Amaze's strings.xml? Hmm.\n\nLet me think of the hint: \"Batch overflow menu: the invert-marking entry is now handled entirely inside the list adapter.\" \"marking\" might be a literal concept: maybe the entry is \"Invert selection\" (márkálás in Hungarian?). The phrase \"invert-marking\" is odd; could be an obfuscated description of the \"Invert selection\" item. If the implementation moved into the list adapter, a bug could be that the menu item is not added by the adapter, hence missing. The QA sweep should check the batch overflow menu and notice the missing \"Invert selection\".\n\nBut is it possible the menu must be opened via a different control? E.g., the selection bottom bar? Here the selection UI has Copy/Delete/Cut buttons and an overflow. That's the batch overflow menu.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the \"invert-marking\" entry only appears in the *per-item* overflow menu when multiple items are selected? No.\n\nLet me look at the per-item \"...\" during selection? In selection mode, the row's properties button remains. Tapping it might show actions for that item. Not \"batch\".\n\nAnother thought: maybe in this build, \"Invert selection\" appears in the *overflow menu of the folder's action bar* when NOT in selection mode? Some apps allow \"Select all\" and \"Invert selection\" from the normal mode menu. Our normal overflow menu on the folder showed Sort/History/Grid View/Hidden Files/Set As Home/Open in Terminal/Exit — no selection options (those are in the top overflow? Actually the normal folder overflow didn't show Select All; the top bar shows only a search/home/overflow). Hmm, where's \"Select All\" outside selection mode? Maybe long-press then overflow.\n\nOK. Let me check Amaze version 3.11.3 behavior from memory: In selection mode, the toolbar has icons for copy, cut, delete (as here) and overflow with:\n- Select all / Deselect all\n- Invert selection? \n- Share\n- Compress\n- Open with\n- Hide\n- Add shortcut\nHmm, I fairly strongly believe \"Invert selection\" is there in recent Amaze. Let me search my memory of Amaze strings: \"invert_selection\" appears in Amaze source? I can recall the bottom sheet in Amaze? Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe I can find evidence on the device: the app's UI strings aren't accessible. But maybe the \"invert\" feature is exposed elsewhere, e.g., long-press selection then in overflow. If the string resource exists but unused, we can't see it.\n\nGiven the strong hint, I'll report missing Invert selection in the batch overflow menu. But before filing, let me reproduce once more in the main folder with a mixed selection (we did: 1 selected → no Invert; all selected → no Invert). That's enough reproduction.\n\nHowever, if the dev says \"expected\" or \"not a bug\", fine. But false positives could be costly? The instructions: \"A confirmed report is logged\". If rejected, presumably not counted. Let's be accurate.\n\nWait, actually, maybe I should look for \"Invert selection\" in the *sort* menu? No.\n\nLet me also test the ability to invert via a different control: Could the entry be in the overflow of the *selection toolbar* but hidden because the device screen is narrow? No, overflow lists all.\n\nLet me now test the remaining changed area: text viewer chunked reading with a truly large file. We need >8KB. Our big.txt is 7301 B. We can increase by copying content? Hmm.\n\nIdea: The text editor has \"Select All\" via long-press? If we select all and copy, then paste multiple times, the file grows exponentially! Yes! In the editor, we can long-press → Select all → Copy → Paste at end? But paste requires moving cursor to end. The IME has a clipboard? We can use the editor's overflow menu which may have \"Select all\" and \"Paste\". Hmm, does the editor have a context menu? Long-press on text shows a floating toolbar with Copy/Paste/Select All. We can: long-press in the EditText → tap \"Select all\" → tap \"Copy\" → then tap at the end? We can't easily move cursor to end of a 7KB text. But we can paste at the beginning: long-press → \"Paste\" (paste at cursor which is at start?) Actually after opening, cursor at 0. Select all then copy then deselect (tap) then paste inserts the entire content at position 0 → doubles the file! Repeat → 14KB, 28KB, 56KB... but each edit+growing might slow.\n\nHmm, clipboard operations might be tricky but doable. However, the selection/copy/paste via on-screen context menu: long_press on the EditText element might select a word and show a toolbar with \"Cut/Copy/Paste/Select all\" (Android's floating toolbar). Our element tool shows it. Let's try in the currently... wait, we're in selection mode in Documents, not editor. We'll need to open big.txt in the editor first.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: use the app's \"Duplicate\"?? Not present.\n\nHmm, maybe easier: use \"Copy\" in file manager to duplicate big.txt many times, then... no concatenation.\n\nWait — the text editor might have an \"Append\" mode? no.\n\nLet's try the editor select-all/copy/paste approach. But first, exit selection mode (Done). We'll go back to the editor.\n\nActually, do we even need a file that large? Let's think about where the chunk bug likely is. The change says \"contents of large text files are now read in chunks rather than all at once.\" If the implementation reads in chunks correctly, there's no bug. The seeded bug might be \"the editor reads only the first chunk\" — observable at chunk-size+1 bytes. Chunk size in such implementations often 1024 or 4096 or 8192. Our 7301-byte file loaded fully (we saw the end marker after reopening). Wait, did we reopen the 7301-byte file? We reopened big.txt when it was 6443 bytes (loaded fully). Then we opened it again and typed CURSOR-TEST and BLOCK2 (so it was loaded with 6443). We haven't reopened since it became 7301. So we know 6443 loads fully. If chunk size is 4096, the bug would have shown at 6443. So chunk size is >6443 (8192 likely or bigger). 7301 < 8192. To test 8192 boundary, need >8192, e.g., 12KB.\n\nSo doubling via copy/paste would get 14.6KB — perfect.\n\nLet's exit selection and do that.\n\nFirst Done [22], then navigate back to QA Renamed. Currently in Documents category page. Press BACK maybe returns to previous folder? Let's use the Done then navigate.\n\nActually we can open big.txt directly from the Documents category (tap big.txt). But in category page, opening text file should work. Let's exit selection, tap big.txt, open editor.\n\nDone tap."}
